# Vietnam — Gulf of Tonkin coverage, August 2–7 1964
#
# This is the canonical Manufacturing-Consent case study. President Johnson
# announced that North Vietnamese boats had attacked U.S. destroyers in
# international waters on the night of August 4, 1964; that attack never
# happened. The U.S. mainstream press largely repeated the administration's
# version as fact and Congress passed the Tonkin Gulf Resolution three days
# later, the legal basis for the next decade of U.S. military escalation.
#
# To run this comparison, replace each placeholder line below with a real
# URL — usually an Internet Archive Wayback Machine snapshot. The
# `--urls-file` workflow expects one URL per line; lines starting with `#`
# are skipped.
#
# Usage (once URLs are filled in):
#   python -m news_lens \
#       --urls-file examples/vietnam_gulf_of_tonkin.urls \
#       --ollama --model qwen3:8b --max-concurrency 1 \
#       --html gulf_of_tonkin.html
#
# Articles to source:
#
# U.S. wire / mainstream:
#   - The New York Times: "U.S. PLANES ATTACK NORTH VIETNAM BASES; PRESIDENT
#     ORDERS 'LIMITED' RETALIATION AFTER COMMUNISTS' PT BOATS RENEW RAIDS"
#     (front page, August 5 1964)
#   - The Washington Post: lead Aug 5 1964 coverage
#   - The AP wire story carried by regional papers Aug 5 1964
#   - Time magazine: cover story August 14 1964
#
# U.S. critical / independent:
#   - I.F. Stone's Weekly, August 24 1964 ("What few know about the Tonkin
#     Bay incidents") — early and lonely contradiction of the official line.
#
# Allied / Western European mainstream:
#   - The Times of London, Aug 5 1964
#   - Le Monde (Paris), Aug 5–7 1964
#
# Soviet / Eastern bloc:
#   - Pravda, Aug 6 1964 (English translation if available)
#
# North Vietnamese:
#   - Nhân Dân (party paper), Aug 1964 statements
#   - Or the Vietnam News Agency dispatches reprinted in Beijing Review
#
# Later-declassified record (NOT for the matrix — for the reader's own
# comparison against the wartime framing):
#   - The 2005 NSA declassification of internal communications confirming
#     the second attack of Aug 4 did not occur (NSA Historical Document
#     Release; available on the National Security Archive site).
#
# Replace each "https://web.archive.org/web/.../..." line with the actual
# Wayback Machine URL once you have located each article. If the Wayback
# Machine has multiple snapshots, prefer one timestamped within a year of
# the article's original publication (older snapshots tend to render less
# of the original layout).
#
# --- replace below this line with one URL per article ---

